const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "LGXNDS Protein Powder Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/lgxnds-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Who owns LGXNDS?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "LGXNDS does not name a founder on its website. A June 2026 federal trademark application lists the owner as Lgxnds Nutrition LLC, a Wyoming company with a mailing address in Stevenson Ranch, California, and claims first use of the brand in October 2023, per the USPTO record. The brand describes itself as California-based and built around the bodybuilding community."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does LGXNDS whey isolate have artificial sweeteners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, by the usual definition. The product page claims zero artificial sweeteners, but the published panels for Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies all list sucralose at the time of writing. Charmies also lists artificial flavor and artificial colors. If you want to avoid sucralose, choose a stevia or monk fruit sweetened isolate instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is LGXNDS protein third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "LGXNDS says every lot is tested for label claims, heavy metals and microbes, including with Analytical Resource Laboratories. We could not find published lot results, and we found no LGXNDS listing in the NSF Certified for Sport database or any Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing. So the testing is the brand's claim, not an independent certification you can check."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein is in LGXNDS Premium Whey Isolate?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Per the labels at the time of writing, Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream and Charmies give 25 g of protein per scoop, and Horchata gives 26 g. Scoops run from 29.8 g to 31.8 g, with 100 to 110 calories. Each 2 lb tub holds about 29 servings, or 30 for vanilla, despite the Amazon listings saying 30 for every flavor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is LGXNDS whey isolate good for lactose intolerance?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Ice Cream use whey isolate as the only protein, which is filtered down to very little lactose, so many lactose-sensitive people tolerate them. Horchata adds non-fat dry milk powder, which contains lactose. The added enzymes are protease and papain, not lactase, so they do not help with lactose. A milk allergy is different: avoid whey entirely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is LGXNDS protein worth the price?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At the $64.99 we saw on Amazon, it costs about $2.15 to $2.24 per 25 g of protein. That is more than Transparent Labs at about $1.93, which is stevia-sweetened and certified, and Dymatize ISO100 at about $1.95 in the 5 lb tub. With the brand's subscription discount it drops to roughly $1.84 to $1.91, which is fair."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What flavors does LGXNDS protein come in?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Amazon sells Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies, a marshmallow cereal flavor, all in 2 lb tubs. The LGXNDS website also lists Strawberry. For the cleanest label, choose Vanilla Ice Cream or Chocolate Milkshake. Charmies adds marshmallow bits with sugar and artificial colors, and Horchata adds non-fat dry milk."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "lgxnds premium whey isolate protein powder",
      "lgxnds protein powder",
      "lgxnds whey isolate",
      "lgxnds protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">LGXNDS Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Dessert flavors, 25 g of protein and a lot of clean-label promises. We checked each flavor's supplement facts panel against those promises, worked out the cost per 25 g, and looked for proof of the testing.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#brand">Who Is Behind LGXNDS</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in the Tub</a>
      <a href="#claims">Claims vs the Label</a>
      <a href="#enzymes">The Digestive Enzymes</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Certification</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#compare">LGXNDS vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          LGXNDS Premium Whey Isolate gives you 25 g of protein in 100 to 110 calories, and the chocolate and vanilla tubs are decent isolates. But the brand's own labels contradict several of its headline claims, we could not find a sport certification, and at about $2.20 per 25 g it costs more than better-tested rivals.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict on LGXNDS
          </h2>

          <p>
            LGXNDS Premium Whey Isolate is a reasonable protein powder wrapped in more marketing than the label can support. The Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Ice Cream tubs deliver what most buyers want: 25 g of protein in 100 to 110 calories, close to zero fat and sugar, and whey protein isolate as the main ingredient, per the supplement facts panels LGXNDS publishes at the time of writing. That part checks out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is the claims. The product page promises zero artificial sweeteners, and the brand's homepage promises no artificial dyes. Every flavor panel we checked lists sucralose. The Charmies flavor adds marshmallow bits colored with Red 40, Yellow 5, Yellow 6 and Blue 1, plus 2 g of added sugar. Horchata lists a protein blend of whey isolate and instant non-fat dry milk powder, which is not what <em>100% isolate, no blends</em> means.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is price and proof. At $64.99 for a 2 lb tub on Amazon when we checked, LGXNDS works out to about $2.15 to $2.24 per 25 g of protein. Transparent Labs costs about $1.93 per 25 g for a stevia-sweetened isolate with Informed Choice certification, and Dymatize ISO100 came in around $1.95 in the 5 lb tub. We found no LGXNDS products in the NSF Certified for Sport database and no Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our ranking is short. If you already like LGXNDS, Vanilla Ice Cream and Chocolate Milkshake are the cleanest flavors, and the subscription price makes them competitive. If you are choosing an isolate from scratch, a better-tested one costs the same or less. We rank on the label, certification status and cost per 25 g. We have not tasted these products. For the whole category ranked side by side, see our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Is Behind LGXNDS
          </h2>

          <p>
            LGXNDS (the name reads as legends, and the brand's slogans use words like Lgxndary) does not name a founder anywhere we could find on its website. Its About page describes a California brand, from Bakersfield to Los Angeles, that grew out of a commitment to the bodybuilding community. The tagline on its product images is LGXNDS aren't born, they're built.
          </p>

          <p>
            The public paper trail is thin but real. A federal trademark application for LGXNDS, filed on June 16, 2026 for dietary and nutritional supplements, lists the owner as Lgxnds Nutrition LLC, a Wyoming limited liability company with a mailing address in Stevenson Ranch, California, according to the USPTO's TSDR record. The filing claims first use of the mark in commerce on October 15, 2023, and the application was still under examination when we checked. That makes LGXNDS a young brand, a couple of years old.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who actually makes the powder is less clear. The About page talks about supplements manufactured in our facility and says that facility is audited every year by UL under the FDA's good manufacturing practice rules. The Our Standards page says the brand partners with a select roster of CGMP and FDA-licensed manufacturers. The isolate's product page says it is made in NSF, GMP and FDA registered facilities in the U.S. None of the pages names the plant.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the range tells you who the brand is for. Next to the isolate sit a stimulant pre-workout, creatine, fat burners, a weight-loss product called GLP-1X, and two products aimed at steroid users: Cycle Defender, sold as cycle support for liver, kidney and heart health, and PCT + Test. That says nothing about what is in the protein tub. It is, though, one more reason a drug-tested athlete should insist on a certified product rather than take a brand's no banned substances line on trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can buy the isolate on the LGXNDS website for $65, on Amazon for about the same, and through a handful of gym-focused supplement stores. The brand advertises a subscription discount and a military discount through GovX. There is only one size, a 2 lb tub, which matters when we get to cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The LGXNDS Flavors Worth Buying, and the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two LGXNDS flavors, cleanest label first, then three alternatives on Amazon: two better-tested isolates and one cheaper one. Label figures come from the supplement facts panels the brands publish at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices are the ones we saw on Amazon at the time of writing and change often. We have not tasted these products, so any taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in the Tub, Flavor by Flavor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amazon sells four flavors: Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies, a marshmallow cereal flavor. The brand's own store also lists Strawberry, though we could not find a published panel for it. Every tub is 907 g (2 lb). The panels differ more than you would expect from one product line, so the table below goes flavor by flavor.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Carbs / sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Worth knowing</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate Milkshake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cocoa, sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla Ice Cream</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">29.8 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leanest panel, sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Horchata</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.8 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g / 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate plus non-fat dry milk, sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Charmies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 2 g added</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Marshmallow bits, artificial colors, gelatin, sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures come from the supplement facts panels on the LGXNDS product page at the time of writing. The Chocolate Milkshake ingredient list reads: whey protein isolate, cocoa powder, natural flavors, sunflower lecithin, a gum blend of acacia, guar and xanthan, silicone (an antifoam), sucralose, salt, a BCAA blend and the digestive enzymes. Vanilla is the same minus the cocoa. Both state that they contain milk, and the chocolate tub notes it is made in a facility that also handles eggs, fish, shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, soy and sesame.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein density looks right for a flavored isolate. Chocolate gives 25 g of protein from a 31.3 g scoop, about 80% protein by weight, and vanilla reaches about 84%. Dymatize ISO100 lands at roughly 78% and Transparent Labs at about 80%, by their own labels. So nothing on the panel hints that the protein is padded out. Without independent test results, though, the label figures are all anyone has.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two smaller points. The panels list about 29 servings per tub (30 for vanilla), while the Amazon listings say 30. And the added BCAA 2:1:1 blend that the product page highlights is 50 mg per scoop. A 25 g serving of whey isolate already carries roughly 5.5 g of BCAAs naturally, by the figure other isolates print on their labels, so the extra 50 mg adds about 1%. It is marketing, not nutrition. LGXNDS does not list leucine per scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where the Marketing and the Label Disagree
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE GAP</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Zero artificial sweeteners, yet sucralose on every panel we checked</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The LGXNDS product page says zero artificial sweeteners, and its Our Standards page promises natural flavors and sweeteners. The published panels for Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies all list sucralose. If you are trying to avoid it, this is not your powder.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Start with the sweetener. Sucralose is a high-intensity sweetener made by chemically modifying sugar, and it is the main thing most shoppers mean when they search for a protein without artificial sweeteners. It is approved as safe to eat and has no calories, so its presence is not a safety issue. The problem is the claim. A buyer who reads the product page and trusts it gets the opposite of what was promised. If you want a truly sucralose-free tub, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> lists stevia and monk fruit options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charmies is the furthest from the clean-label pitch. Its panel lists marshmallow bits made from sugar, corn syrup, modified food starch, dextrose and gelatin, colored with Red 40, Yellow 6, Blue 1 and Yellow 5, with artificial flavor. That adds up to 2 g of added sugar and 3 g of carbohydrate per scoop. Meanwhile the brand's homepage says no artificial dyes, and the product page says 0 g of added sugar. The gelatin also matters if you avoid animal gelatin but eat dairy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Horchata raises a different issue. Its panel lists a 29.78 g protein blend of whey protein isolate and instant non-fat dry milk powder. Non-fat dry milk is roughly half lactose and carries casein, so it is not isolate. The panel's 1 g of total sugar suggests the milk powder is a small share of the scoop. Even so, a product marketed as 100% premium whey isolate, no blends, should not list a blend. If you are lactose-sensitive, stick to chocolate or vanilla.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest is sloppy copy rather than anything serious. The Amazon titles say 0 g of fat, while the chocolate panel lists 0.5 g. The Charmies Amazon title says 26 g of protein, while the brand's panel says 25 g. The brand page describes the product as having a Chocolate Milkshake flavor whichever flavor you select. None of this is harmful, but protein is a trust purchase. You cannot check the contents at home, so when the claims you can check are wrong, weigh the claims you cannot check, like lot testing, accordingly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="enzymes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Digestive Enzymes: Helpful or Label Dressing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The digestive enzymes are the main thing LGXNDS uses to set its isolate apart. Each scoop lists 10 mg of protease rated at 1,000 HUT per gram and 10 mg of papain rated at 50 TU per gram. Do the math and that is about 10 HUT of protease activity and half a unit of papain activity per scoop. Standalone digestive enzyme supplements commonly list protease activity in the thousands or tens of thousands of HUT per dose, so this is a small amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            The evidence for adding proteases to protein powder is thin. A few small studies, mostly funded by enzyme suppliers, have found modest increases in blood amino acids after a protein shake with a specific branded enzyme blend at that blend's own dose. They do not show that a 10 mg sprinkle prevents bloating or builds more muscle. Your own digestive system already breaks whey down efficiently, which is why whey is called fast-digesting in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger gap is lactose. If dairy bloats you, lactose is the usual reason, and the blend contains no lactase, the enzyme that breaks lactose down. Protease and papain digest protein, not milk sugar. What actually helps a lactose-sensitive drinker is the isolate itself, which is filtered down to very little lactose. That benefit is real, and it is true of every whey isolate, not just this one. Our guide to <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> explains the difference, and our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> picks go further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some bloating comes from elsewhere. The gum blend of acacia, guar and xanthan thickens the shake, and in larger amounts guar and xanthan can cause gas in sensitive people. Some people also report stomach upset from sucralose, though the amount in one scoop is small. If a scoop bothers you, try half a scoop, mix it with water rather than milk, or switch to an unflavored isolate with no gums or sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            One safety note: whey isolate is low in lactose, but it is still a milk protein. It is not safe for anyone with a milk allergy, whatever enzymes are added. If you have a diagnosed allergy, or a digestive condition you are managing with a doctor, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certification: What We Could and Could Not Verify
          </h2>

          <p>
            LGXNDS makes a lot of testing claims. Its site banner says third-party lab tested. The isolate's page says every lot is tested for label claims, heavy metals and microbial contaminants, and that the brand regularly tests with Analytical Resource Laboratories. The homepage adds testing for 75 pesticides, four heavy metals, 16 contaminants and nine microbial contaminants, and the Our Standards page describes third-party screening of raw materials as they arrive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is what we could not find. There were no certificates of analysis or lot results for the isolate posted on the LGXNDS website at the time of writing. A search of the NSF Certified for Sport database returned no LGXNDS products. We found no Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing, and we did not find any independent published test result for the powder. So the testing claims may well be true, but they are the brand's word.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters. When a brand pays a contract lab to test its lots, the brand sees the results and decides what to publish. A certification program like NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport tests lots on its own schedule, screens for substances banned in sport, and lists certified products in a public database anyone can search. Note too that an NSF-registered facility is a factory registration for manufacturing practices. It is not a certification of the product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drug-tested athletes should rule LGXNDS out for now and buy from the <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> list instead. For everyone else, the main contaminant worry with protein powder is heavy metals. Whey isolates generally test lower than many plant and chocolate-heavy powders, and our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> covers which brands publish their results.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like LGXNDS and want reassurance, ask. The brand lists info@lgxnds.com on its contact page. Send the lot number from your tub and ask for the certificate of analysis. A company that tests every lot should be able to send one, and if it will not, that tells you something too.
          </p>
        </div>

