        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy LGXNDS, Who Should Skip It, and What Buyers Say
          </h2>

          <p>
            Independent taste feedback on LGXNDS is scarce. The reviews on the brand's own site and on retailer pages are glowing, praising a smooth, creamy texture and no bloating, but those are seller-hosted pages, and we found little independent discussion beyond them. Treat flavor as a bit of a gamble. Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Ice Cream are the safest bets, while Horchata and Charmies are novelty flavors you either love or regret. The gum blend makes the shake a little thicker, so start with about 8 to 10 oz of cold water or milk per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy LGXNDS if</strong> you like dessert-style flavors, want a 100 to 110 calorie shake with 25 g of protein, do not need a sport certification, and can get it at the subscription price. At about $55 a tub it is a fair deal for a flavored isolate, and the chocolate and vanilla panels are perfectly respectable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete, you avoid sucralose, you avoid artificial colors (Charmies), you are lactose-sensitive and were eyeing Horchata, or you avoid gelatin (Charmies again). Skip it too if you buy on price, since a 5 lb isolate is cheaper per gram and LGXNDS only comes in 2 lb tubs. Our pick in most of those cases is Transparent Labs, or Nutricost if cost comes first.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you buy it, use it like any whey isolate. One scoop after training or between meals is the obvious use, but what matters most is your total daily protein, not timing. Many lifters aim for about 1.6 g per kg of body weight per day, and our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> works through the numbers. Keep the lid sealed and the tub somewhere cool and dry, and see <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">does protein powder expire</a> for how long an opened tub lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, the usual caveat. Protein powder is food, not medicine, and this review is not medical advice. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, have a milk allergy, or take medication, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a high-protein supplement to your diet.
          </p>
