              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  LGXNDS does not name a founder on its website. A June 2026 federal trademark application lists the owner as Lgxnds Nutrition LLC, a Wyoming company with a mailing address in Stevenson Ranch, California, and claims first use of the brand in October 2023, per the USPTO record. The brand describes itself as California-based and built around the bodybuilding community.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does LGXNDS whey isolate have artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, by the usual definition. The product page claims zero artificial sweeteners, but the published panels for Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies all list sucralose at the time of writing. Charmies also lists artificial flavor and artificial colors. If you want to avoid sucralose, choose a stevia or monk fruit sweetened isolate instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is LGXNDS protein third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  LGXNDS says every lot is tested for label claims, heavy metals and microbes, including with Analytical Resource Laboratories. We could not find published lot results, and we found no LGXNDS listing in the NSF Certified for Sport database or any Informed Sport or Informed Choice listing. So the testing is the brand's claim, not an independent certification you can check.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in LGXNDS Premium Whey Isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per the labels at the time of writing, Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream and Charmies give 25 g of protein per scoop, and Horchata gives 26 g. Scoops run from 29.8 g to 31.8 g, with 100 to 110 calories. Each 2 lb tub holds about 29 servings, or 30 for vanilla, despite the Amazon listings saying 30 for every flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is LGXNDS whey isolate good for lactose intolerance?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Ice Cream use whey isolate as the only protein, which is filtered down to very little lactose, so many lactose-sensitive people tolerate them. Horchata adds non-fat dry milk powder, which contains lactose. The added enzymes are protease and papain, not lactase, so they do not help with lactose. A milk allergy is different: avoid whey entirely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is LGXNDS protein worth the price?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At the $64.99 we saw on Amazon, it costs about $2.15 to $2.24 per 25 g of protein. That is more than Transparent Labs at about $1.93, which is stevia-sweetened and certified, and Dymatize ISO100 at about $1.95 in the 5 lb tub. With the brand's subscription discount it drops to roughly $1.84 to $1.91, which is fair.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What flavors does LGXNDS protein come in?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Amazon sells Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Ice Cream, Horchata and Charmies, a marshmallow cereal flavor, all in 2 lb tubs. The LGXNDS website also lists Strawberry. For the cleanest label, choose Vanilla Ice Cream or Chocolate Milkshake. Charmies adds marshmallow bits with sugar and artificial colors, and Horchata adds non-fat dry milk.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

